CODE Semaphore-Based Barrier Implementation
============================================

  A barrier is a common primitive in parallel computing. It acts as a point of
  timing synchronization among all of the threads in a group. When a thread
  arrives at a specific point in its code, the "barrier", it must wait until
  all other threads have arrived at that same point as well. Once all threads
  have reached this barrier point, they all may proceed with their execution.

  The most straightforward way to implement a barrier is as a function (or in
  this lab, a method within a class). Every thread calls this method, which
  blocks the thread until all other threads have also called the same method.
  Once every thread has called the barrier method, all threads are unblocked.

  Your task is to implement such a barrier in the file 'sema_barrier.py'. Begin
  by studying the code in 'sema_barrier.py' Note that the size of the thread
  group using the barrier is defined as 'N_THREADS' at the top of the file.

  The code defines a 'Barrier' class with a 'wait' method. This method is what
  gets called by a group of threads and should block each caller until every
  member of the group has called the method.

  The existing code starts 'N_THREADS' additional threads from the initial main
  thread and then waits for all of these threads to complete. Meanwhile, the
  function executed by each thread prints out a message, calls the barrier's
  'wait' method, and then prints out a second message before terminating.

  Your task is to fill in the missing code in the following locations:

    - In the 'Barrier' class constructor. Initialize the necessary
      synchronization primitives here.
    - In the body of the 'wait' method. Use the synchronization primitives you
      initialized earlier to implement the barrier blocking/unblocking behavior
      described above.

  Hints:

    - You will need both a lock and a semaphore for the Barrier class. The lock
      protects reads/writes of the barrier's internal state, and the semaphore
      is used to block and unblock threads as necessary.
    - Normally, Python's 'with' keyword is great for locks and semaphores.
      However, in this situation, it's actually better to manually control when
      these primitives are acquired and released.

QUIZ Questions 'sema_barrier' Observations
==========================================

  What should be the initial value of the semaphore in your barrier?
    - ( ) 0
    - ( ) 1
    - ( ) N_THREADS
    - ( ) None of the Above

  Each new thread is given a unique index, and each of these threads prints its
  index to the screen before it arrives at the barrier. What do you observe
  about the order in which these threads execute when you run the program
  multiple times?
    - ( ) The threads always get turns in the order in which they were created.
    - ( ) The threads always get turns in the reverse order in which they were
          created.
    - ( ) The threads don't get turns on the CPU in any particular order.
    - ( ) The threads get turns in a random order, but that order is consistent
          across multiple runs of the program.

  Each thread also prints out its ID after getting through the barrier. What do
  you observe about the order in which threads get to leave the barrier when
  you run the program multiple times?
    - ( ) The threads always get to leave the barrier in the same order in
          which they first arrived at the barrier.
    - ( ) The threads leave the barrier in the reverse of the order in which
          they arrived at the barrier.
    - ( ) The threads don't get to leave the barrier in any particular order.
    - ( ) The threads leave the barrier in a random order, but the order is
          consistent across multiple runs of the program.
